{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++ code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <utility>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nusing pii = pair<int, int>;\nusing vps = vector<pair<int, string>>;\n\nstring input;\nvps candidates;\n\nvoid explore(int pos, int adjustment) {\n    if (pos == (int)candidates.size()) {\n        size_t first = input.find('@');\n        size_t last = input.rfind('@');\n        if (first != string::npos && first == last) {\n            cout << input << '\\n';\n            exit(0);\n        }\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    int idx = candidates[pos].first + adjustment;\n    const string& replacement = candidates[pos].second;\n    \n    if (replacement == \"@\") {\n        input.replace(idx, 2, \"@\");\n        explore(pos + 1, adjustment - 1);\n        input.replace(idx, 1, \"at\");\n    } else {\n        input.replace(idx, 3, \".\");\n        explore(pos + 1, adjustment - 2);\n        input.replace(idx, 1, \"dot\");\n    }\n    explore(pos + 1, adjustment);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n    \n    cin >> input;\n    int n = input.length();\n    for (int i = 1; i + 2 < n; ++i) {\n        if (input.substr(i, 3) == \"dot\")\n            candidates.push_back({i, \".\"});\n        else if (input.substr(i, 2) == \"at\")\n            candidates.push_back({i, \"@\"});\n    }\n    explore(0, 0);\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nstring input;\nvector<pair<int, string>> candidates;\nstring best_result;\nint min_length = INT_MAX;\n\nbool isValid(const string& s) {\n    // Must contain exactly one '@'\n    int atCount = 0;\n    for (char c : s) {\n        if (c == '@') atCount++;\n    }\n    if (atCount != 1) return false;\n    \n    // Must not start or end with '@' or '.'\n    if (s[0] == '@' || s.back() == '@' || s[0] == '.' || s.back() == '.') return false;\n    \n    // Must contain only '.', '@', and lowercase Latin letters\n    for (char c : s) {\n        if (!((c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || c == '.' || c == '@')) return false;\n    }\n    \n    // Check that '@' is not at the beginning or end\n    size_t atPos = s.find('@');\n    if (atPos == 0 || atPos == s.length() - 1) return false;\n    \n    // Check that there's at least one character before and after '@'\n    if (atPos == 0 || atPos == s.length() - 1) return false;\n    \n    return true;\n}\n\nvoid explore(int pos, int adjustment) {\n    if (pos == (int)candidates.size()) {\n        if (isValid(input)) {\n            if (input.length() < min_length || \n                (input.length() == min_length && input < best_result)) {\n                min_length = input.length();\n                best_result = input;\n            }\n        }\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    int idx = candidates[pos].first + adjustment;\n    const string& replacement = candidates[pos].second;\n    \n    // Try replacement\n    if (replacement == \"@\") {\n        input.replace(idx, 2, \"@\");\n        explore(pos + 1, adjustment - 1);\n        input.replace(idx, 1, \"at\");\n    } else {\n        input.replace(idx, 3, \".\");\n        explore(pos + 1, adjustment - 2);\n        input.replace(idx, 1, \"dot\");\n    }\n    \n    // Try no replacement\n    explore(pos + 1, adjustment);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n    \n    cin >> input;\n    int n = input.length();\n    \n    // Find all possible \"dot\" and \"at\" occurrences\n    for (int i = 0; i + 2 < n; ++i) {\n        if (input.substr(i, 3) == \"dot\") {\n            candidates.push_back({i, \".\"});\n        }\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < n; ++i) {\n        if (input.substr(i, 2) == \"at\") {\n            candidates.push_back({i, \"@\"});\n        }\n    }\n    \n    best_result = \"\";\n    explore(0, 0);\n    \n